net/tap-win32: make set_link on the netdev set the TAP's media status

A TAP-Windows adapter reports media connected only while an application
holds it open and has said so with TAP_IOCTL_SET_MEDIA_STATUS.
tap_win32_open() says so once, and nothing changes it afterwards: set_link
flips the guest NIC's link, while Windows keeps seeing the adapter
connected for as long as QEMU runs.

That matters when the TAP is bridged to a physical NIC. A Windows bridge
is connected if any of its members is, so a TAP held connected keeps the
bridge connected after the physical member loses carrier. Windows then
keeps the bridge's address, default route and DNS servers through a
pulled cable or a dropped Wi-Fi link. With two uplinks, the system
resolver keeps asking through the dead bridge. On reconnection it sends
no DHCP request, because it saw no media change, so a move to another
network keeps the old address.

Implement link_status_changed for the tap-win32 backend and set the media
status from the netdev's own link state. A host process that watches the
physical member can then mirror its carrier with set_link on the netdev,
and Windows sees the bridge go down and come back with it. set_link on
the guest NIC still changes only the guest's view: the hook is called
then too, but it reads the netdev's link, which that path leaves alone.

Measured with a host process holding the TAP's media status in QEMU's
place, on a bridge of a TAP and a wired NIC under Windows 11 Home 24H2
(build 26100), with the media status mirroring the wired member: a cable
pulled and replugged is followed by an INIT-REBOOT renewal, where nothing
was sent before; with two uplinks, outbound traffic and DNS move to the
other one within 5 s of the pull; a move to another subnet takes a new
address in 11 s.

--- a/net/tap-win32.c
+++ b/net/tap-win32.c
@@ -704,11 +704,26 @@
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * The netdev's link is also the TAP adapter's media status on the host, so
+ * that set_link on the netdev is seen by Windows as well as by the guest.
+ */
+static void tap_win32_link_status_changed(NetClientState *nc)
+{
+    TAPState *s = DO_UPCAST(TAPState, nc, nc);
+
+    if (!tap_win32_set_status(s->handle->handle, !nc->link_down)) {
+        warn_report("tap: could not set the media status of %s: error %lu",
+                    nc->name, GetLastError());
+    }
+}
+
 static NetClientInfo net_tap_win32_info = {
     .type = NET_CLIENT_DRIVER_TAP,
     .size = sizeof(TAPState),
     .receive = tap_receive,
     .cleanup = tap_cleanup,
+    .link_status_changed = tap_win32_link_status_changed,
 };
 
 static int tap_win32_init(NetClientState *peer, const char *model,
